  ($qb$S06C10Q03$qb$, $qb$S06$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Correlation between management quality and team performance in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Measured and used — data links manager effectiveness to team performance outcomes$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged — the relationship is understood but not formally measured$qb$, $qb$Suspected but unquantified — the link is believed to exist but no data is used to demonstrate it$qb$, $qb$Not considered — the relationship between manager quality and team performance is not analysed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S06C10Q04$qb$, $qb$S06$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Performance data in your organisation informs decisions about:$qb$, $qb$Structure, resource allocation, development investment, and talent strategy simultaneously$qb$, $qb$Some of these — performance data informs 1–2 of these decisions$qb$, $qb$One of these — performance data is used for one specific purpose only$qb$, $qb$None of these — performance data is not used to inform organisational decisions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S06C10Q05$qb$, $qb$S06$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The quality of performance data in your organisation (accuracy, completeness, timeliness) is:$qb$, $qb$High — data is trusted by leaders and managers and is used with confidence$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — some data quality issues exist but the data is broadly usable$qb$, $qb$Variable — data quality varies significantly by function and manager$qb$, $qb$Poor — performance data quality problems undermine its usefulness for decision-making$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S06C10Q06$qb$, $qb$S06$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Lessons from performance management (what worked, what did not, how to improve) are incorporated into the next performance cycle:$qb$, $qb$Systematically — a formal review of the performance management process occurs and improvements are implemented$qb$, $qb$Partially — obvious improvements are made but the process is not systematically reviewed$qb$, $qb$Rarely — the same performance management approach is repeated year after year without meaningful improvement$qb$, $qb$Never — performance management process learning does not occur$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S06C10Q07$qb$, $qb$S06$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Exit interview data in your organisation is used to identify performance management failures or gaps:$qb$, $qb$Yes — exit interview themes related to performance management are tracked and used to improve$qb$, $qb$Partially — exit data is collected but not systematically used to improve performance management$qb$, $qb$Rarely — exit data is collected but filed rather than used$qb$, $qb$No — exit interview data does not feed back into performance management improvement$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S06C10Q08$qb$, $qb$S06$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Benchmarking of performance management effectiveness against external best practice is:$qb$, $qb$Regular — the organisation actively learns from external performance management research and peer practices$qb$, $qb$Occasional — some benchmarking occurs but it is not systematic$qb$, $qb$Rare — the organisation rarely looks externally for performance management improvement ideas$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — performance management practices are never compared to external standards$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S06C10Q09$qb$, $qb$S06$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The performance management system in your organisation has been updated in the last 3 years:$qb$, $qb$Yes — it has been meaningfully updated based on evidence of what works and employee/manager feedback$qb$, $qb$Partially — some elements have been updated but the core approach is unchanged$qb$, $qb$Minimally — superficial changes have been made but the fundamental approach is the same$qb$, $qb$No — the performance management system is essentially the same as it was 3+ years ago$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S06C10Q10$qb$, $qb$S06$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Overall, performance data in your organisation is used to:$qb$, $qb$Drive meaningful improvement in both individual and organisational performance$qb$, $qb$Support management conversations and reporting$qb$, $qb$Fulfill administrative and compliance requirements$qb$, $qb$No clear purpose — data is collected but not used to drive outcomes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true);
INSERT INTO question_bank (question_id, section_code, category_code, level_tag, text, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, score_a, score_b, score_c, score_d, active) VALUES
  ($qb$S07C01Q01$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The culture around learning and skill development in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — learning is valued, visibly supported by leadership, and woven into daily work$qb$, $qb$Positive — learning is encouraged and some investment exists but it is not deeply embedded$qb$, $qb$Weak — learning is mentioned but not prioritised or resourced meaningfully$qb$, $qb$Absent — learning is not a cultural value and most employees do not develop professionally at work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C01Q02$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Leaders in your organisation model continuous learning behaviour (publicly acknowledge gaps, learn new skills, share insights):$qb$, $qb$Yes — leaders visibly and authentically demonstrate that they are still learning$qb$, $qb$Some do — certain leaders model learning; others project an image of already-knowing$qb$, $qb$Rarely — leadership behaviour suggests that appearing competent matters more than actual learning$qb$, $qb$No — leadership does not model learning; status and hierarchy depend on the impression of expertise$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C01Q03$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation are given time during work hours to learn and develop:$qb$, $qb$Yes — protected learning time is part of the work schedule$qb$, $qb$Partly — some employees have learning time; others cannot find space in their workloads$qb$, $qb$Nominally — learning time is encouraged but workload pressure makes it effectively unavailable$qb$, $qb$No — learning is expected to happen outside work hours with no protected time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C01Q04$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Mistakes and failures in your organisation are treated as:$qb$, $qb$Learning opportunities — failures are analysed, lessons are shared, and the insights inform future decisions$qb$, $qb$Useful when managed well — failures are sometimes explored but this is not a consistent practice$qb$, $qb$Individually absorbed — mistakes are managed quietly without organisational learning$qb$, $qb$Punished — the consequence of failure discourages risk-taking and prevents honest learning$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C01Q05$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Learning investment per employee (training budget, tools, programmes) in your organisation relative to size and revenue is:$qb$, $qb$Above average — the organisation invests significantly in people development$qb$, $qb$Average — investment is present but not a strategic differentiator$qb$, $qb$Below average — L&D budget is minimal and often the first cut$qb$, $qb$Near zero — effective learning investment does not exist$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C01Q06$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Peer-to-peer learning and knowledge sharing across teams in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Structured and encouraged — learning communities, internal talks, and knowledge bases exist and are actively used$qb$, $qb$Informal but present — colleagues share knowledge but it is not structured$qb$, $qb$Rare — silos prevent effective peer learning$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — employees learn in isolation and knowledge does not flow across teams$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C01Q07$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The learning and development function (L&D or HR) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strategic and respected — L&D is a valued business partner aligned to organisational capability needs$qb$, $qb$Functional — programmes exist and are used though not deeply strategic$qb$, $qb$Administrative — L&D primarily manages compliance training and scheduling$qb$, $qb$Non-existent or negligible — there is no meaningful L&D function$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C01Q08$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The appetite for trying new approaches and methods in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — experimentation is encouraged and the organisation is genuinely curious about new ways of working$qb$, $qb$Moderate — new ideas are welcome but implementation is cautious$qb$, $qb$Low — new approaches face significant resistance or inertia$qb$, $qb$Very low — the dominant culture is to defend existing methods regardless of evidence$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C01Q09$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Learning outcomes (measurable skill gain, performance improvement linked to development) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Tracked and reported — the business impact of learning investment is measured$qb$, $qb$Partially tracked — some measurement exists but it is not comprehensive$qb$, $qb$Not tracked — training activities are recorded but outcomes are not measured$qb$, $qb$Not considered — no connection is made between learning investment and business results$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C01Q10$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The overall trajectory of organisational capability in your organisation over the last 2 years is:$qb$, $qb$Improving significantly — the organisation is measurably more capable than it was$qb$, $qb$Improving steadily — gradual capability improvement is visible$qb$, $qb$Flat — capability levels have not meaningfully changed$qb$, $qb$Declining — the organisation is losing capability faster than it is building it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C02Q01$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Your organisation has a structured annual learning plan aligned to business strategy:$qb$, $qb$Yes — learning priorities are defined by the strategy, budgeted, and tracked against outcomes$qb$, $qb$Partially — some structured planning exists but it is not comprehensive or strategy-aligned$qb$, $qb$Minimally — a training calendar exists but is not linked to capability priorities$qb$, $qb$No — training is sourced ad hoc without a structured annual plan$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C02Q02$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Leadership development programmes in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Robust and regular — multi-level programmes prepare leaders at every stage$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some leadership development programmes exist for senior levels$qb$, $qb$Limited — occasional workshops are offered without a coherent curriculum$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — leadership development is not a formal organisational investment$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C02Q03$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Technical and functional training (job-specific skills) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Comprehensive and current — all functions have structured training aligned to role requirements$qb$, $qb$Adequate for most roles — key technical skills are covered but gaps exist$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — training availability varies significantly by function$qb$, $qb$Poor — most employees develop technical skills informally without structured support$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C02Q04$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Soft skills development (communication, collaboration, problem-solving, critical thinking) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Structured and integrated — behavioural skills are developed alongside technical ones$qb$, $qb$Partially developed — some soft skills programmes exist but they are not systematic$qb$, $qb$Minimal — soft skills are assumed rather than developed$qb$, $qb$Absent — the organisation does not invest in developing behavioural capabilities$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C02Q05$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Learning delivery methods (in-person, online, blended, coaching) used in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Diverse and chosen for effectiveness — methods are selected based on learning outcome, not habit$qb$, $qb$Reasonably varied — a mix of methods is used$qb$, $qb$Limited — the organisation relies on one or two delivery methods regardless of the learning need$qb$, $qb$Very limited — learning delivery is almost entirely classroom or self-study$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C02Q06$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Compliance and mandatory training in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Efficient, current, and completion-tracked — compliance training does not crowd out developmental learning$qb$, $qb$Managed — compliance training is completed but occasionally displaces developmental learning$qb$, $qb$Heavy — compliance training burden significantly limits time available for development$qb$, $qb$Dominant — mandatory training consumes most of the available L&D capacity$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C02Q07$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Mentoring and coaching programmes (structured, not informal) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Active — formal mentoring and coaching is available and widely used$qb$, $qb$Available — programmes exist but uptake is limited$qb$, $qb$Informal only — mentoring happens through personal relationships, not structured programmes$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — no formal mentoring or coaching programmes are in place$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C02Q08$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Learning programmes in your organisation are evaluated for effectiveness:$qb$, $qb$Rigorously — impact is measured at behaviour change and business results level$qb$, $qb$At satisfaction level only — participant ratings are collected but not outcome data$qb$, $qb$Not formally — programmes run without evaluation$qb$, $qb$Never — there is no learning evaluation practice$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C02Q09$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$New manager development (for people newly promoted to management roles) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Comprehensive — new managers receive structured support for the transition to people management$qb$, $qb$Partial — some support is offered but it is not sufficient for the role complexity$qb$, $qb$Minimal — new managers receive brief onboarding but not meaningful development support$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — managers are appointed and expected to figure it out$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C02Q10$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$External learning (conferences, external courses, industry programmes) is:$qb$, $qb$Actively supported with budget and time — employees at multiple levels access external learning$qb$, $qb$Supported for senior employees — external learning is primarily available at leadership level$qb$, $qb$Rarely supported — external learning happens at individual initiative with limited organisational support$qb$, $qb$Not supported — external learning is neither funded nor encouraged$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C03Q01$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Individual Development Plans (IDPs) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$In place for all employees — IDPs are a standard management practice, reviewed regularly$qb$, $qb$In place for senior employees — IDPs exist at senior level but not across the organisation$qb$, $qb$Nominally present — IDP templates exist but are not seriously used$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — individual development planning is not a management practice$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C03Q02$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The quality and depth of development plan conversations between managers and employees in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — managers invest meaningfully in understanding each person's growth trajectory$qb$, $qb$Adequate — conversations happen but are often brief and surface-level$qb$, $qb$Poor — development conversations are checkbox activities rather than genuine planning$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — managers and employees do not have meaningful development conversations$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C03Q03$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Career path visibility (understanding what advancement requires and looks like) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Clear — employees know what they need to do to progress and the paths are transparent$qb$, $qb$Partially clear — some career paths are visible; others are not$qb$, $qb$Opaque — career advancement is poorly understood and perceived as relationship-dependent$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — career paths are not defined and advancement feels arbitrary$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C03Q04$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employees who complete development activities in your organisation experience:$qb$, $qb$Career benefit — development leads to promotion, new opportunity, or visible performance improvement$qb$, $qb$Skill improvement — capability grows though career benefit is not always immediate$qb$, $qb$Limited benefit — development activities are undertaken but do not visibly influence careers$qb$, $qb$No benefit — employees question whether development effort produces any organisational return$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C03Q05$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Development activities in your organisation are prioritised for:$qb$, $qb$All employees — development is available and encouraged at every level$qb$, $qb$High potentials — development is concentrated on identified future leaders$qb$, $qb$Senior employees — development is primarily available at management and leadership level$qb$, $qb$Almost no one — development is not a meaningful investment for the majority$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C03Q06$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Stretch assignments and high-visibility projects as development tools in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Systematically used — managers deliberately assign challenging work to develop people$qb$, $qb$Sometimes used — some managers use stretch assignments intentionally; others do not$qb$, $qb$Rarely used — assignments are made for delivery, not for development$qb$, $qb$Not considered as development tools — assignments are entirely task-driven$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C03Q07$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Development plans in your organisation are aligned to both individual aspiration and organisational need:$qb$, $qb$Yes — IDPs balance personal career goals with the skills the organisation needs to build$qb$, $qb$Partially — organisation need sometimes dominates without enough attention to individual aspiration$qb$, $qb$Weakly — development is driven by individual aspiration with little connection to organisational capability needs$qb$, $qb$No — IDPs exist in isolation from both individual career goals and organisational priorities$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C03Q08$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$MM$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation are held accountable for the development of their team members:$qb$, $qb$Yes — manager effectiveness includes development as a measured outcome$qb$, $qb$Partially — development is discussed as a manager responsibility but not formally assessed$qb$, $qb$Informally — the expectation exists but no accountability mechanism is in place$qb$, $qb$No — manager accountability does not include the development of their people$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C03Q09$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Internal promotion rates (the proportion of open roles filled by existing employees) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$High — the organisation successfully develops and promotes from within$qb$, $qb$Moderate — a meaningful proportion of roles are filled internally$qb$, $qb$Low — most roles are filled externally due to insufficient internal capability$qb$, $qb$Very low — internal promotion is rare and employees know advancement requires leaving$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C03Q10$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The development of diverse talent (women, underrepresented groups) into senior roles in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Actively managed — specific development investments are made to build diverse leadership pipelines$qb$, $qb$Partially addressed — diversity in development is discussed but not consistently delivered$qb$, $qb$Aspirational — commitments are made but representation in senior development programmes does not reflect diversity goals$qb$, $qb$Not addressed — development programmes do not specifically address diversity pipeline challenges$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C04Q01$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Digital literacy (the ability to use digital tools effectively for work) across the general employee population in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — most employees are digitally capable and adapt readily to new tools$qb$, $qb$Moderate — digital literacy is adequate for current needs but would benefit from investment$qb$, $qb$Low — digital skill gaps are visible and affect productivity$qb$, $qb$Very low — digital literacy deficiency is a significant operational and strategic constraint$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C04Q02$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Technology upskilling programmes (training employees on new systems and digital tools) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Proactive — employees are trained before new technology is deployed, not after$qb$, $qb$Reactive — training follows deployment and employees adapt under pressure$qb$, $qb$Minimal — employees are expected to learn new technology on their own$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — technology is deployed without meaningful training support$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C04Q03$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Data literacy (the ability to read, interpret, and use data for decisions) among non-technical employees in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Good — employees across functions can interpret dashboards and use data in their work$qb$, $qb$Adequate for basic needs — most employees can use standard reports but not analyse data independently$qb$, $qb$Limited — data usage is confined to specialists; most employees work without data engagement$qb$, $qb$Very limited — data illiteracy is a significant barrier to data-driven management$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C04Q04$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The organisation's investment in developing the digital skills needed for the next 3–5 years is:$qb$, $qb$Proactive and substantial — future digital skill requirements are mapped and investment is planned$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some future digital skill investment is in place$qb$, $qb$Minimal — digital skill investment is reactive and short-term$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — future digital skill needs are not planned for$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C04Q05$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation who resist or struggle with technology adoption are:$qb$, $qb$Supported with training and encouragement — no one is left behind in digital transitions$qb$, $qb$Generally supported — help is available though not always proactively provided$qb$, $qb$Left to manage on their own — individuals who struggle are expected to catch up independently$qb$, $qb$Penalised or bypassed — technology laggards are disadvantaged or excluded$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C04Q06$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Cybersecurity awareness and digital safety practices among employees in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Strong — regular training and testing ensure employees are vigilant$qb$, $qb$Adequate — baseline training exists and most employees are aware$qb$, $qb$Weak — cybersecurity training happens but awareness is not consistently high$qb$, $qb$Very weak — cybersecurity practices are inadequate and represent a material risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C04Q07$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The pace at which your organisation adopts new technology tools relative to its industry peers is:$qb$, $qb$Fast — the organisation is typically an early adopter and uses technology as a competitive advantage$qb$, $qb$Average — the organisation adopts technology at a similar pace to peers$qb$, $qb$Slow — the organisation tends to be a late adopter and technology gaps are periodically visible$qb$, $qb$Very slow — technology adoption lags significantly and this is a recognised competitive disadvantage$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C04Q08$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Cloud, SaaS, and modern productivity platforms (e.g. Microsoft 365, Google Workspace, Slack, Notion) are used in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Broadly and effectively — modern platforms are the norm and employees are proficient$qb$, $qb$Partially — some modern platforms are in use but adoption is incomplete$qb$, $qb$Minimally — legacy systems dominate and modern platforms are not widely available$qb$, $qb$Not at all — the organisation relies on outdated technology and this limits productivity$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C04Q09$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Change management capability to support technology transitions (communication, training, adoption support) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — technology changes are managed with structured change support that drives adoption$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some change support is provided for significant technology changes$qb$, $qb$Weak — technology is deployed with minimal change management$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — technology changes are announced and implemented without structured adoption support$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C04Q10$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The ROI of technology investment in your organisation (whether technology actually improves productivity and capability) is:$qb$, $qb$Measured and positive — technology investments are evaluated and consistently deliver value$qb$, $qb$Partially measured — some technology ROI is assessed but not comprehensively$qb$, $qb$Not measured — technology is deployed without evaluation of business impact$qb$, $qb$Negative — technology investment in this organisation has frequently failed to deliver expected value$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C05Q01$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$AI literacy (understanding what AI can and cannot do) among employees in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — most employees understand AI concepts and can apply basic AI tools in their work$qb$, $qb$Growing — awareness is increasing and some employees are skilled AI users$qb$, $qb$Low — AI literacy is confined to a small number of specialists$qb$, $qb$Very low — AI is not understood by the majority and adoption is minimal$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C05Q02$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The organisation's strategy for AI adoption is:$qb$, $qb$Clear and implemented — an AI adoption roadmap exists with named initiatives, owners, and timelines$qb$, $qb$Defined — strategy exists on paper but implementation is slow$qb$, $qb$Developing — AI strategy conversations are happening but no clear plan exists$qb$, $qb$Absent — AI adoption is not strategically planned in this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C05Q03$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$AI tools are currently being used productively in your organisation for:$qb$, $qb$Multiple core functions — AI is embedded in several business processes and delivering measurable value$qb$, $qb$A few functions — AI is producing value in 1–2 areas but has not been broadly deployed$qb$, $qb$Pilot activities — AI is being tested in limited pilots without broad adoption$qb$, $qb$No meaningful work yet — AI tool use is negligible or limited to personal experimentation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C05Q04$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation who use AI tools in their work report that AI:$qb$, $qb$Meaningfully improves their speed, quality, or output — AI adoption is producing real productivity gains$qb$, $qb$Sometimes helps — AI is useful in some contexts but not consistently$qb$, $qb$Adds limited value currently — the tools are available but the benefit is unclear$qb$, $qb$Has not been provided or is not accessible to most employees$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C05Q05$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Leadership in your organisation understands and champions AI adoption:$qb$, $qb$Yes — senior leaders understand AI's potential, model its use, and champion adoption actively$qb$, $qb$Partially — some leaders are engaged; others are passive or uninformed$qb$, $qb$Weakly — AI is acknowledged as important but leadership does not actively drive adoption$qb$, $qb$No — leadership is not informed about or engaged in AI adoption$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C05Q06$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Ethical AI practices (bias awareness, data privacy, responsible use frameworks) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Formally addressed — an AI ethics framework exists and guides AI deployment decisions$qb$, $qb$Partially addressed — some awareness exists but governance is not comprehensive$qb$, $qb$Minimally addressed — ethical considerations are mentioned but not structured into AI adoption$qb$, $qb$Not addressed — AI is being deployed without ethical governance$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C05Q07$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$AI-related anxiety among employees (fear of job displacement, concern about AI in their role) is:$qb$, $qb$Actively managed — leadership communicates openly about AI's role and how it will affect the workforce$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged — some communication exists but it does not fully address employee concerns$qb$, $qb$Largely unaddressed — employees have concerns but leadership has not meaningfully engaged with them$qb$, $qb$Very high and unmanaged — AI anxiety is causing significant disengagement and talent risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C05Q08$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The speed at which your organisation is building genuine AI capability relative to its industry peers is:$qb$, $qb$Fast — the organisation is ahead of peers in AI capability and sees it as a competitive advantage$qb$, $qb$At pace — broadly aligned with industry adoption rates$qb$, $qb$Slow — the organisation is behind peers and the gap is widening$qb$, $qb$Very slow — AI capability is negligible and the organisation risks being significantly left behind$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C05Q09$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Investment in AI training and upskilling across the employee population in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Significant — structured AI literacy and tool training is being rolled out across functions$qb$, $qb$Growing — some AI training investment is occurring$qb$, $qb$Minimal — AI upskilling is limited to specialist roles$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — no meaningful AI training investment has been made$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C05Q10$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The integration of AI into human workflows (human-AI collaboration) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-designed — AI augments human work without replacing human judgement inappropriately$qb$, $qb$Developing — some integration is in place but human-AI workflow design is incomplete$qb$, $qb$Ad hoc — AI use is individual rather than designed into team or organisational workflows$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — AI is not integrated into how work is done$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C06Q01$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Knowledge management (capturing, storing, and sharing organisational knowledge) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Systematic — knowledge bases, playbooks, and documentation systems are in place and used$qb$, $qb$Partial — some knowledge is captured but significant expertise lives in people's heads$qb$, $qb$Minimal — documentation exists for compliance but operational knowledge is not systematically captured$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation relies almost entirely on tacit knowledge held by individuals$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
