  ($qb$S07C09Q05$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Creative problem-solving, design thinking, and innovation skills in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Actively developed — structured programmes build these capabilities across relevant functions$qb$, $qb$Present in pockets — some employees have received development in these areas$qb$, $qb$Rare — these skills are valued but not developed$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — structured development in creative problem-solving does not occur$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C09Q06$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The organisation's approach to building resilience and wellbeing capability (not just support, but skill) in employees is:$qb$, $qb$Structured — resilience skills are developed as part of management and employee programmes$qb$, $qb$Partial — some resilience content is included in development programmes$qb$, $qb$Minimal — wellbeing is supported but resilience as a skill is not developed$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — resilience skill building is not part of the development programme$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C09Q07$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The organisation's external environment scanning for capability signals (industry reports, competitor moves, technology trends) that should inform learning priorities is:$qb$, $qb$Systematic — external capability signals are regularly reviewed and feed directly into the L&D agenda$qb$, $qb$Occasional — some external scanning influences learning priorities but it is not structured$qb$, $qb$Rare — learning priorities are set internally without significant external input$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the L&D agenda is not informed by external capability intelligence$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C09Q08$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The development of future-ready capability in diverse employee groups (women, younger employees, underrepresented groups) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Deliberately inclusive — future skills programmes specifically ensure diverse populations are included$qb$, $qb$Partially inclusive — most programmes are open to all but active inclusion is not always ensured$qb$, $qb$Weakly inclusive — future skills development inadvertently favours certain groups$qb$, $qb$Not inclusive — access to future skills development is unevenly distributed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C09Q09$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Strategic workforce planning (modelling future headcount, skills, and structure needs) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Sophisticated — workforce planning models inform both hiring and development strategy$qb$, $qb$Basic — some planning exists but it is not granular or scenario-based$qb$, $qb$Minimal — high-level headcount planning exists but skill and capability modelling does not$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not systematically plan its future workforce composition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C09Q10$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The overall future-readiness of your organisation's capability portfolio (the skills it has vs. the skills it will need) is:$qb$, $qb$High — the organisation is actively building the capabilities it will need$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some future-ready investment exists alongside a heavy focus on current capability$qb$, $qb$Low — the organisation is focused on today's needs with limited investment in tomorrow's capabilities$qb$, $qb$Very low — the capability gap between current and future requirements is widening$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C10Q01$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The Learning Management System (LMS) or equivalent learning technology in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Modern and well-adopted — the platform is used effectively for programme delivery, tracking, and reporting$qb$, $qb$Functional — an LMS exists and is used but has limitations$qb$, $qb$Outdated — the LMS is past its useful life and constrains the L&D function$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — there is no learning technology infrastructure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C10Q02$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Personalised learning (content and pathways tailored to individual role, level, and gap) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Available and used — personalised learning paths are generated based on role, capability, and aspiration$qb$, $qb$Partially available — some personalisation exists for specific programmes$qb$, $qb$Limited — most learning is cohort-based without meaningful personalisation$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — all employees receive the same generic learning content$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C10Q03$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Mobile and on-demand learning (learning available when employees need it, on any device) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-established — employees access learning on demand and find it useful$qb$, $qb$Available — on-demand content exists but adoption is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Limited — learning is primarily scheduled and not available on demand$qb$, $qb$Not available — learning requires scheduled attendance or desk-based access only$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C10Q04$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The use of AI in learning design and delivery (AI-powered content, adaptive learning, AI coaching tools) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Active — AI is being used to personalise learning, generate content, or provide coaching support$qb$, $qb$Being explored — pilots or evaluations are in progress$qb$, $qb$On the agenda — awareness exists but no deployment has occurred$qb$, $qb$Not on the agenda — AI in learning is not being considered$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C10Q05$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Microlearning (short, focused learning moments embedded in workflow) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Widely used — employees regularly engage with short-form learning as part of their daily work$qb$, $qb$Available but underused — content exists but uptake is low$qb$, $qb$Limited — most learning is long-form programmes rather than microlearning$qb$, $qb$Not used — the organisation has not adopted microlearning approaches$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C10Q06$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Social learning (learning from peers through communities, forums, shared content) is enabled by technology in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Well-supported — platforms and communities exist and are actively used for peer learning$qb$, $qb$Partially supported — some social learning technology exists but adoption is limited$qb$, $qb$Minimally supported — social learning happens informally without technology enablement$qb$, $qb$Not supported — no technology exists to facilitate social learning$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C10Q07$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Virtual Reality (VR), simulation, or immersive learning for high-stakes skills (safety, leadership scenarios, technical procedures) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$In use where appropriate — immersive learning is deployed for relevant high-stakes skills$qb$, $qb$Being evaluated — interest exists but deployment has not occurred$qb$, $qb$Not in use — conventional training methods are used for all skill types$qb$, $qb$Not considered — emerging learning technology is not on the L&D agenda$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C10Q08$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Learning analytics (data on who is learning, what is effective, and what is being skipped) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Rich and used — learning data drives decisions about content, programme design, and prioritisation$qb$, $qb$Basic — completion data is available but deeper analytics are limited$qb$, $qb$Minimal — only compliance completion data is tracked$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — no learning analytics are available$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C10Q09$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Investment in L&D technology relative to the size and complexity of the organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Appropriate and increasing — technology investment matches the scale and ambition of the learning agenda$qb$, $qb$Moderate — adequate for current needs but not at the frontier$qb$, $qb$Below what is needed — technology limitations constrain L&D effectiveness$qb$, $qb$Very low — L&D technology is a known constraint on organisational learning capability$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S07C10Q10$qb$, $qb$S07$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Overall, the L&D technology and innovation capability in your organisation positions it to:$qb$, $qb$Lead — the organisation's learning infrastructure is a competitive differentiator in talent attraction and development$qb$, $qb$Compete adequately — the infrastructure supports a functional learning agenda$qb$, $qb$Survive — the infrastructure is just sufficient but is not a source of advantage$qb$, $qb$Fall behind — L&D technology gaps are widening the capability gap between this organisation and its peers$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true);
INSERT INTO question_bank (question_id, section_code, category_code, level_tag, text, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, score_a, score_b, score_c, score_d, active) VALUES
  ($qb$S08C01Q01$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Voluntary attrition rate in your organisation over the last 12 months is:$qb$, $qb$Below industry benchmark — attrition is a competitive strength$qb$, $qb$At industry benchmark — attrition is manageable$qb$, $qb$Above industry benchmark — attrition is a recognised problem$qb$, $qb$Significantly above benchmark — attrition is a crisis costing the organisation heavily$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C01Q02$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Attrition data in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Tracked monthly, reported at leadership level, and analysed by department, level, and tenure$qb$, $qb$Tracked annually and reviewed at leadership level$qb$, $qb$Tracked but not regularly reviewed or analysed$qb$, $qb$Not tracked in any systematic way$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C01Q03$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Attrition cost (recruitment, onboarding, productivity loss) is understood in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Quantified — cost per attrition event is calculated and used in business decisions$qb$, $qb$Estimated — leadership knows it is significant but has not quantified it precisely$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged in principle but not calculated$qb$, $qb$Not considered — the financial cost of attrition is not tracked or discussed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C01Q04$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Attrition trends (which departments, levels, tenures are losing people) are analysed in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Deeply — root cause analysis by segment drives specific retention interventions$qb$, $qb$Broadly — trends are identified but interventions are not always targeted$qb$, $qb$Superficially — overall numbers are tracked without segment analysis$qb$, $qb$Not at all — attrition is tracked as a total number only$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C01Q05$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Predictive attrition risk (identifying who is likely to leave before they resign) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Active — risk signals are monitored and proactive retention conversations happen$qb$, $qb$Partially active — some managers identify flight risks informally$qb$, $qb$Reactive — attrition risk is only identified after resignation$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation is consistently surprised by departures$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C01Q06$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Attrition benchmarks for your industry and talent segment are known to leadership:$qb$, $qb$Yes — leadership actively monitors competitor attrition and talent market trends$qb$, $qb$Partially — some benchmarking occurs but it is not comprehensive$qb$, $qb$Rarely — benchmarks are checked occasionally without ongoing monitoring$qb$, $qb$No — the organisation does not benchmark its attrition against peers$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C01Q07$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The board or senior leadership reviews attrition data:$qb$, $qb$Quarterly — attrition is a standing agenda item at leadership level$qb$, $qb$Annually — reviewed during performance cycle$qb$, $qb$Only when it becomes a visible crisis$qb$, $qb$Never — attrition data is not reported at board or senior leadership level$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C01Q08$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$First-year attrition (employees leaving within 12 months of joining) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Low and tracked — early attrition is measured and addressed through onboarding and hiring improvements$qb$, $qb$Tracked and acknowledged but not fully addressed$qb$, $qb$Known to be high but not systematically managed$qb$, $qb$Not separately tracked from overall attrition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C01Q09$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Regretted attrition (losing people the organisation wanted to keep) vs unregretted attrition is tracked separately:$qb$, $qb$Yes — the distinction is made and regretted attrition drives specific retention actions$qb$, $qb$Informally — managers know which departures are regretted but it is not formally tracked$qb$, $qb$Not clearly distinguished — all attrition is treated the same$qb$, $qb$Not tracked — no distinction is made between regretted and unregretted departures$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C01Q10$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The organisation's response time from identifying an attrition risk to taking retention action is:$qb$, $qb$Fast (within 1–2 weeks) — flight risks get immediate management attention$qb$, $qb$Moderate (2–4 weeks) — action eventually happens but not always quickly enough$qb$, $qb$Slow (over a month) — by the time action is taken, the employee has often mentally left$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — attrition risk identification does not lead to structured retention action$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C02Q01$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Exit interviews in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Conducted for all leavers by a neutral party (HR or external) — results are analysed and acted on$qb$, $qb$Conducted for most leavers — results are collected but not consistently actioned$qb$, $qb$Conducted inconsistently — some managers conduct them; others do not$qb$, $qb$Not conducted — there is no exit interview process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C02Q02$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The quality of data gathered from exit interviews in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — structured questions produce actionable insight that informs retention strategy$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some useful themes emerge but depth is limited$qb$, $qb$Low — exit interviews are superficial and produce little useful data$qb$, $qb$Very low — exit interviews are a formality with no real insight gathered$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C02Q03$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Exit interview data in your organisation is analysed for patterns:$qb$, $qb$Regularly — themes are identified, reported to leadership, and drive management action$qb$, $qb$Occasionally — patterns are noticed over time but reporting is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Rarely — data is collected but not systematically analysed$qb$, $qb$Never — exit interview responses are filed without analysis$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C02Q04$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The offboarding process in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Structured and respectful — leavers receive a clear checklist, proper handover time, and a dignified farewell$qb$, $qb$Adequate — the basics are covered but the experience is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Minimal — offboarding is primarily administrative with little attention to the human element$qb$, $qb$Poor — employees leave without a proper handover or farewell process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C02Q05$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employees who leave your organisation are treated with:$qb$, $qb$Consistent dignity and appreciation — exit is handled respectfully regardless of circumstances$qb$, $qb$Generally well — most exits are handled professionally$qb$, $qb$Variably — treatment at exit depends on the relationship with the manager$qb$, $qb$Poorly — some exits are handled in ways that damage the organisation's reputation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C02Q06$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Knowledge transfer when an employee leaves your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Structured — a formal handover period and documentation process is standard$qb$, $qb$Partially structured — key knowledge is transferred for senior roles but not consistently$qb$, $qb$Informal — handover depends on the individual's goodwill$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — employees regularly leave taking critical knowledge with them$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C02Q07$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Counter-offer practices in your organisation (offering departing employees more to stay) are:$qb$, $qb$Strategic — counter-offers are only made when the departure is genuinely regretted and the root cause is addressed$qb$, $qb$Reactive — counter-offers are made impulsively without addressing underlying issues$qb$, $qb$Rarely — the organisation rarely makes counter-offers$qb$, $qb$Never — once an employee resigns, no retention attempt is made$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C02Q08$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The notice period and handover experience in your organisation leaves the departing employee and remaining team:$qb$, $qb$Well-prepared — handover is complete, the team is supported, and the transition is smooth$qb$, $qb$Adequately managed — some gaps exist but the transition is generally handled$qb$, $qb$Poorly managed — handover is rushed and the remaining team is left with significant gaps$qb$, $qb$Very poorly managed — departures regularly create operational crises$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C02Q09$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Alumni relationships with former employees in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Actively maintained — an alumni network exists and former employees are seen as brand ambassadors$qb$, $qb$Informally maintained — some managers stay in touch with former employees$qb$, $qb$Not maintained — once employees leave, the relationship ends$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — no effort is made to maintain relationships with former employees$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C02Q10$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The offboarding process in your organisation is reviewed and improved:$qb$, $qb$Regularly — offboarding quality is assessed and improved based on leaver and manager feedback$qb$, $qb$Occasionally — improvements are made when obvious problems arise$qb$, $qb$Rarely — the same offboarding process has been used for years without review$qb$, $qb$Never — offboarding is not treated as a process to be managed or improved$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C03Q01$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Your organisation has a deliberate retention strategy:$qb$, $qb$Yes — a documented retention strategy with specific initiatives, owners, and budgets exists$qb$, $qb$Partially — retention is discussed at leadership level and some initiatives are in place$qb$, $qb$Informally — retention is managed through ad hoc responses to departures$qb$, $qb$No — there is no deliberate retention strategy; attrition is accepted as inevitable$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C03Q02$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Retention initiatives in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Targeted — different initiatives address different segments (high performers, critical roles, specific levels)$qb$, $qb$Broad — general initiatives exist but are not segment-specific$qb$, $qb$Generic — one-size-fits-all approaches without targeting$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — no specific retention initiatives are in place$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C03Q03$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The primary driver of employee departure in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$External opportunity — people leave for advancement, not because of internal problems$qb$, $qb$A mix of external pull and internal push — some avoidable attrition exists$qb$, $qb$Primarily internal — management quality, culture, or compensation are the dominant departure drivers$qb$, $qb$Largely internal and unaddressed — known retention problems are not being fixed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C03Q04$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Pay competitiveness as a retention tool in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Actively managed — salaries are reviewed against market annually and adjusted proactively$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — pay is reviewed but some market drift occurs$qb$, $qb$Below market — pay is a known retention risk that is not fully addressed$qb$, $qb$Significantly below market — pay is a primary driver of attrition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C03Q05$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Career development as a retention tool in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees can see a clear path forward and feel they are growing$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some career development exists but not consistently enough to retain ambitious people$qb$, $qb$Weak — limited career opportunity is a significant retention risk$qb$, $qb$Very weak — absence of growth opportunity is a primary driver of departure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C03Q06$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Manager quality as a retention factor in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Recognised and managed — manager quality is a deliberate retention lever$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged — the link between manager quality and retention is known but not fully actioned$qb$, $qb$Underestimated — manager problems drive attrition without sufficient management intervention$qb$, $qb$Ignored — poor manager behaviour persists despite being a known attrition driver$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C03Q07$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Retention conversations (proactive discussions with valued employees about their future) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Standard practice — managers regularly check in with high performers about their plans and needs$qb$, $qb$Occasional — some managers have retention conversations; others do not$qb$, $qb$Rare — retention conversations only happen after someone threatens to leave$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — managers do not proactively discuss retention with their teams$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C03Q08$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Flexible working as a retention tool in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-leveraged — flexibility is a genuine competitive advantage in retaining talent$qb$, $qb$Used where possible — flexibility is offered but not consistently or as a strategic tool$qb$, $qb$Limited — inflexibility around working arrangements is a retention disadvantage$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not offer flexibility and this is costing it talent$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C03Q09$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The organisation's ability to retain its highest performers is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — top performers stay significantly longer than average performers$qb$, $qb$Moderate — most high performers stay but some exceptional talent is lost$qb$, $qb$Weak — high performers leave at similar rates to average performers$qb$, $qb$Very weak — the best people leave the fastest$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C03Q10$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$ROI of retention initiatives in your organisation (cost of retention vs cost of replacement) is:$qb$, $qb$Measured and positive — investment in retention is demonstrably cheaper than replacement costs$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged but not quantified — retention is valued without formal ROI measurement$qb$, $qb$Not measured — retention investment and replacement cost are not compared$qb$, $qb$Not considered — there is no business case framework for retention investment$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C04Q01$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Critical role identification (knowing which roles the organisation can least afford to lose) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Formal and current — critical roles are documented, reviewed annually, and drive succession investment$qb$, $qb$Informal — senior leaders know which roles are critical but it is not formally documented$qb$, $qb$Ad hoc — criticality is only recognised when a role becomes vacant$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — no systematic identification of critical roles exists$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C04Q02$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Single points of failure (roles with no internal backup) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Identified and actively being addressed — succession plans or cross-training are in place$qb$, $qb$Known but not fully addressed — most single points of failure are acknowledged$qb$, $qb$Numerous and unaddressed — the organisation has many single points of failure$qb$, $qb$Unknown — the organisation has not assessed its single-point-of-failure exposure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
