  ($qb$S08C08Q07$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Investment in wellbeing as a retention tool in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strategic and measured — wellbeing investment is linked to retention outcomes$qb$, $qb$Moderate — wellbeing programmes exist and are used$qb$, $qb$Minimal — wellbeing investment is token and does not meaningfully address the drivers of burnout$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — wellbeing is not invested in as a retention lever$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C08Q08$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$The recovery time available to employees in your organisation after intense periods of work is:$qb$, $qb$Adequate and protected — downtime after peaks is built into planning and respected$qb$, $qb$Generally available — recovery time exists but is sometimes pressured$qb$, $qb$Insufficient — there is little recovery between peaks; the high pace is continuous$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation moves immediately from one intense period to the next$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C08Q09$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Absence due to stress, burnout, or mental health in your organisation as a proportion of total absence is:$qb$, $qb$Low and declining — wellbeing investment is reducing stress-related absence$qb$, $qb$Moderate and stable — stress-related absence is present but not escalating$qb$, $qb$High and rising — stress-related absence is increasing and is a known problem$qb$, $qb$Very high — stress-related absence is a significant operational and financial problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C08Q10$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall, the organisation's management of wellbeing and burnout as drivers of attrition is:$qb$, $qb$Effective — the organisation is successfully preventing burnout-driven departure$qb$, $qb$Partially effective — some prevention occurs but burnout-driven attrition still happens$qb$, $qb$Ineffective — burnout is a known attrition driver that is not adequately addressed$qb$, $qb$Very ineffective — burnout is one of the primary causes of attrition in this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C09Q01$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Your organisation's policy on rehiring former employees (boomerang hires) is:$qb$, $qb$Open and active — former employees are welcomed back and the organisation maintains contact with alumni$qb$, $qb$Open in principle — rehires are accepted but no active alumni engagement exists$qb$, $qb$Case-by-case — rehires are evaluated individually without a consistent policy$qb$, $qb$Closed — former employees are not considered for rehire$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C09Q02$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Former employees who return to your organisation (boomerang hires) perform:$qb$, $qb$Very well — boomerang hires typically ramp up faster and have better retention$qb$, $qb$Well — comparable to new external hires$qb$, $qb$Variably — some perform well; others do not$qb$, $qb$Poorly — the organisation has had negative experiences with boomerang hires$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C09Q03$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$An alumni network or community for former employees of your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Exists and is actively maintained — alumni are engaged, informed, and treated as brand assets$qb$, $qb$Exists informally — some alumni maintain connections but without organisational structure$qb$, $qb$Is being built — plans exist but the network is not yet functional$qb$, $qb$Does not exist — former employees have no structured connection with the organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C09Q04$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Former employees refer clients, partnerships, or candidates to your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Regularly — alumni are active sources of business and talent referrals$qb$, $qb$Occasionally — some referrals come from former employees$qb$, $qb$Rarely — alumni are not a meaningful source of referrals$qb$, $qb$Never — the organisation has no alumni engagement to generate referrals$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C09Q05$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The exit experience in your organisation (how people leave) affects their likelihood to return or refer:$qb$, $qb$Positively — most people leave on good terms and retain positive feelings about the organisation$qb$, $qb$Neutrally — exits are professional but not relationship-preserving$qb$, $qb$Negatively for some — certain exit experiences damage the alumni relationship$qb$, $qb$Very negatively — poor exit experiences mean most alumni are not advocates or potential returners$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C09Q06$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Alumni of your organisation are used as:$qb$, $qb$Multiple assets — brand ambassadors, referral sources, clients, and rehire candidates$qb$, $qb$Occasional referral sources — alumni are contacted when specific needs arise$qb$, $qb$Not used — the organisation does not actively leverage its alumni network$qb$, $qb$Avoided — the organisation has no engagement with former employees$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C09Q07$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The organisation's approach to staying connected with high-performing former employees is:$qb$, $qb$Proactive — leadership maintains personal relationships with key alumni$qb$, $qb$Informal — some managers stay in touch personally$qb$, $qb$Reactive — the organisation reconnects with alumni only when a need arises$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — former employees are not tracked or engaged with$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C09Q08$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Data on former employees (contact details, last role, reason for leaving) is maintained in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Yes — a clean, current alumni database is maintained$qb$, $qb$Partially — some data is retained but it is not systematically maintained$qb$, $qb$Minimally — some historical data exists but is not current$qb$, $qb$No — no data on former employees is maintained after departure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C09Q09$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The time investment in rehiring a boomerang employee compared to a new external hire is:$qb$, $qb$Significantly lower — reduced screening, faster onboarding, proven cultural fit$qb$, $qb$Somewhat lower — some efficiency is gained but screening is still thorough$qb$, $qb$Similar — boomerang hires go through the same process as new hires$qb$, $qb$More difficult — rehires face additional scrutiny that makes the process slower$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C09Q10$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Overall, the organisation's alumni strategy and rehire approach:$qb$, $qb$Creates a competitive advantage — alumni are a genuine source of talent, business, and brand value$qb$, $qb$Generates some value — alumni occasionally provide useful referrals or return as employees$qb$, $qb$Has little impact — alumni relations are not systematically leveraged$qb$, $qb$Is absent — the organisation has no alumni strategy$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C10Q01$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The organisational culture as experienced by employees in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$A retention strength — employees stay because of the culture as much as any other factor$qb$, $qb$Generally positive but not a differentiating retention factor$qb$, $qb$Mixed — culture is a reason some people stay and a reason others leave$qb$, $qb$A retention problem — the culture is a primary driver of voluntary departure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C10Q02$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Psychological safety (the ability to speak up, make mistakes, and be oneself) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees feel genuinely safe and this is a retention advantage$qb$, $qb$Moderate — safety exists in most areas with some pockets of concern$qb$, $qb$Low — psychological unsafety is a significant factor in employee dissatisfaction and departure$qb$, $qb$Very low — fear is a dominant culture feature and is directly driving attrition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C10Q03$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The sense of belonging and community in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees feel a genuine sense of community that makes them want to stay$qb$, $qb$Adequate — employees feel reasonably connected but community is not a differentiating factor$qb$, $qb$Weak — many employees feel isolated or disconnected, contributing to attrition$qb$, $qb$Very weak — absence of belonging is a primary driver of departure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C10Q04$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Recognition culture in your organisation as a retention factor is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees feel seen and appreciated and this influences their decision to stay$qb$, $qb$Moderate — recognition happens but is not consistently strong enough to retain$qb$, $qb$Weak — poor recognition is a frequently cited dissatisfaction and attrition factor$qb$, $qb$Very weak — the absence of recognition is actively driving people to leave$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C10Q05$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Inclusion and fairness in your organisation as retention factors are:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees feel included and fairly treated regardless of background$qb$, $qb$Moderate — the organisation is broadly fair with some areas for improvement$qb$, $qb$Weak — perceptions of unfairness or exclusion are contributing to attrition in specific groups$qb$, $qb$Very weak — discrimination or systemic unfairness is a significant attrition driver$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C10Q06$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The alignment between the organisation's stated values and its actual behaviour as a retention factor is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees trust the organisation to live its values and this creates loyalty$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some alignment exists but employees notice gaps$qb$, $qb$Weak — significant gaps between stated values and lived reality reduce trust and loyalty$qb$, $qb$Very weak — values hypocrisy is a major driver of cynicism and departure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C10Q07$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Leadership credibility as a retention factor in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees trust and respect leadership and this keeps them committed$qb$, $qb$Moderate — leadership credibility is generally adequate$qb$, $qb$Weak — lack of confidence in leadership is contributing to attrition$qb$, $qb$Very weak — leadership credibility has collapsed and is a primary departure driver$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C10Q08$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Team dynamics and peer relationships as retention factors in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Strong — people stay because they value their team and colleagues$qb$, $qb$Adequate — team relationships are professional and reasonably positive$qb$, $qb$Weak — poor team dynamics are contributing to attrition$qb$, $qb$Very poor — toxic team environments are directly driving people out$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C10Q09$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The organisation's purpose and meaning as a retention factor is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees feel their work matters and this creates loyalty$qb$, $qb$Moderate — purpose is present but not a primary retention driver$qb$, $qb$Weak — absence of meaning is contributing to disengagement and attrition$qb$, $qb$Very weak — the organisation has no compelling purpose and employees feel no loyalty to its mission$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S08C10Q10$qb$, $qb$S08$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall, the cultural and emotional experience of working in your organisation creates:$qb$, $qb$High retention pull — most employees feel emotionally committed and would not easily leave$qb$, $qb$Adequate retention — employees are reasonably satisfied but not deeply committed$qb$, $qb$Low retention pull — culture is not keeping people and is sometimes pushing them out$qb$, $qb$A retention crisis — the cultural experience is a primary driver of voluntary departure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true);
INSERT INTO question_bank (question_id, section_code, category_code, level_tag, text, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, score_a, score_b, score_c, score_d, active) VALUES
  ($qb$S09C01Q01$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The organisation's sales strategy is:$qb$, $qb$Clearly defined, documented, and understood by the whole team$qb$, $qb$Generally understood at leadership level but not fully cascaded$qb$, $qb$Vague — the team has a general direction without a clear strategy$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation sells reactively without any defined strategy$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C01Q02$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Ideal Customer Profile (ICP) definition in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Precise, documented, and actively used to prioritise sales effort$qb$, $qb$Defined but not consistently applied in day-to-day decisions$qb$, $qb$Informal — a shared understanding exists but is not documented$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation sells to anyone willing to buy$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C01Q03$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Market segmentation in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Sophisticated — markets segmented by value, potential, and fit with resources allocated accordingly$qb$, $qb$Adequate — broad segments identified and effort broadly aligned$qb$, $qb$Basic — segmentation exists in name but resource allocation does not follow it$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — all prospects receive the same level of attention$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C01Q04$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Competitive positioning in your organisation's sales approach is:$qb$, $qb$Clear and compelling — the team can articulate why customers should choose over alternatives$qb$, $qb$Adequate — a value proposition exists but not always compelling competitively$qb$, $qb$Weak — the team struggles to differentiate from competitors$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — no defined competitive position$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C01Q05$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Annual revenue targets in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Ambitious, data-driven, and cascaded clearly to individuals with accountability$qb$, $qb$Set at leadership level and broadly communicated without full cascade$qb$, $qb$Set but not effectively cascaded to individual accountability$qb$, $qb$Not set — the organisation operates without clear revenue targets$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C01Q06$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Sales territory or account planning in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Rigorous — territories planned for maximum coverage with individual account plans$qb$, $qb$Adequate — territories defined and some account planning occurs$qb$, $qb$Basic — territories exist but account plans are not consistently developed$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — no structured territory or account planning$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C01Q07$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Pricing strategy in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Value-based and disciplined — pricing reflects value delivered and discounting is controlled$qb$, $qb$Generally sound — pricing is reasonable and broadly maintained$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — pricing varies by salesperson and discounting is common$qb$, $qb$Reactive — driven by what the customer will pay rather than by value$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C01Q08$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The alignment between marketing and sales strategy in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Tight — marketing generates qualified leads that sales can close from the same strategy$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some alignment exists but gaps cause friction$qb$, $qb$Weak — marketing and sales operate largely independently$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — marketing and sales are siloed and blame each other$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C01Q09$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Sales investment relative to revenue targets in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-calibrated — resource is appropriately sized and invested for the targets set$qb$, $qb$Adequate — investment broadly supports the targets$qb$, $qb$Underfunded — the team is expected to achieve targets with insufficient resource$qb$, $qb$Significantly underfunded — targets bear no relationship to investment behind them$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C01Q10$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The sales strategy in your organisation is reviewed and updated:$qb$, $qb$Quarterly — strategy is a live document adjusted to conditions and performance$qb$, $qb$Annually — reviewed once a year$qb$, $qb$Only when performance problems emerge$qb$, $qb$Never — the sales strategy has not been meaningfully updated in years$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C02Q01$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Lead generation in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Multi-channel, structured, and producing a consistent flow of qualified leads$qb$, $qb$Functional — leads are generated but the process is not fully systematic$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — lead generation is sporadic and dependent on individual effort$qb$, $qb$Poor — the organisation struggles to generate sufficient qualified leads$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C02Q02$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Inbound lead generation (content, SEO, digital marketing, events) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — inbound is a significant and growing proportion of the pipeline$qb$, $qb$Developing — some inbound activity exists but contribution to pipeline is limited$qb$, $qb$Minimal — the organisation has limited inbound capability$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation relies entirely on outbound for leads$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C02Q03$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Outbound prospecting (cold outreach, referrals, partnerships) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Systematic and disciplined — structured with clear activity metrics and accountability$qb$, $qb$Active but inconsistent — outbound happens without consistent process or measurement$qb$, $qb$Minimal — done ad hoc when pipeline is low$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not do systematic outbound$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C02Q04$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Lead qualification in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Rigorous — a clear qualification framework is used consistently by all salespeople$qb$, $qb$Adequate — most salespeople qualify reasonably well$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — qualification standards vary widely across the team$qb$, $qb$Poor — the team pursues poorly qualified opportunities, wasting time and reducing win rates$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C02Q05$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The quality of leads passed from marketing to sales in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — marketing leads convert to opportunities at a predictable and competitive rate$qb$, $qb$Moderate — marketing leads are useful but not consistently well-qualified$qb$, $qb$Variable — some marketing leads are valuable; many are not worth pursuing$qb$, $qb$Poor — marketing leads are consistently low quality$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C02Q06$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Pipeline coverage (ratio of pipeline value to revenue target) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong (3x or above) — the pipeline provides sufficient coverage to achieve targets$qb$, $qb$Adequate (2-3x) — coverage is sufficient but does not provide much buffer$qb$, $qb$Thin (1-2x) — pipeline coverage is insufficient and creates target risk$qb$, $qb$Very thin (below 1x) — achieving targets requires converting almost all current opportunities$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C02Q07$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$CRM usage for tracking leads and pipeline in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Disciplined — all leads and opportunities are logged and maintained in real time$qb$, $qb$Generally good — CRM is used but data quality and completeness vary$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some salespeople use the CRM properly; others do not$qb$, $qb$Poor — the CRM is not trusted or consistently used as the source of pipeline truth$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C02Q08$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The time from initial lead contact to first qualified meeting in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Short and disciplined — the team moves quickly from lead to engagement$qb$, $qb$Reasonable — leads are followed up within an acceptable timeframe$qb$, $qb$Slow — lead follow-up is inconsistent and opportunities are sometimes lost through delay$qb$, $qb$Very slow — the organisation consistently fails to capitalise on leads in a timely manner$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C02Q09$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Referral and partner-generated leads as a proportion of total pipeline is:$qb$, $qb$High and growing — referrals and partnerships are a strategic and productive lead source$qb$, $qb$Moderate — referrals contribute meaningfully but are not a primary channel$qb$, $qb$Low — referral and partner leads are occasional rather than systematic$qb$, $qb$Negligible — the organisation does not generate significant leads through referrals or partners$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C02Q10$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The organisation's ability to generate pipeline consistently month-over-month is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — pipeline generation is a reliable organisational capability, not individual-dependent$qb$, $qb$Moderate — pipeline is reasonably consistent but dependent on a few key performers$qb$, $qb$Variable — pipeline varies significantly from month to month$qb$, $qb$Highly variable — the organisation experiences feast and famine cycles in pipeline generation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C03Q01$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The sales process in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Defined, documented, and consistently followed — all salespeople follow the same process stages$qb$, $qb$Defined but inconsistently followed — the process exists but individual variation is high$qb$, $qb$Informal — there is a general understanding of how to sell but no documented process$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — each salesperson operates independently with no common process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C03Q02$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Win rates (proportion of qualified opportunities converting to closed business) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Above industry average and improving — the sales process is producing competitive win rates$qb$, $qb$At industry average — win rates are acceptable$qb$, $qb$Below average — win rates indicate sales process or capability gaps$qb$, $qb$Well below average — poor win rates are a significant revenue problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C03Q03$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Sales cycle length in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-managed and predictable — average sales cycle is known and actively compressed$qb$, $qb$Generally known — cycle length is tracked but not consistently managed$qb$, $qb$Variable — sales cycle length varies widely and is not well-managed$qb$, $qb$Unknown — the organisation does not track or manage sales cycle length$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C03Q04$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Proposal and presentation quality in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Consistently high — proposals are tailored, professional, and compelling$qb$, $qb$Generally good — proposals are adequate but not always highly customised$qb$, $qb$Variable — proposal quality depends heavily on the individual salesperson$qb$, $qb$Poor — proposals are generic, low quality, or not fit for purpose$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S09C03Q05$qb$, $qb$S09$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Objection handling capability in your organisation's sales team is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — salespeople are trained and common objections are prepared for$qb$, $qb$Adequate — most salespeople handle objections reasonably well$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some salespeople handle objections well; others struggle$qb$, $qb$Weak — poor objection handling is a known sales capability gap$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
