  ($qb$S10C08Q01$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the crisis communication planning and readiness is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — a crisis communication plan exists, is tested, and senior leaders know their roles$qb$, $qb$Adequate — a basic plan exists but has not been tested or practised$qb$, $qb$Weak — the organisation knows it needs a crisis plan but does not have one$qb$, $qb$Very poor — there is no crisis communication preparation whatsoever$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q02$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the track record of managing public reputational crises is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — past crises have been managed well and reputation was preserved or fully recovered$qb$, $qb$Adequate — crises have been managed without major lasting damage$qb$, $qb$Weak — some crises have been well-managed; others have caused lasting reputational damage$qb$, $qb$Very poor — past crises have damaged reputation in ways that have not been recovered$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q03$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the real-time monitoring of brand threats is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — brand monitoring tools track online sentiment, competitor activity, and adverse media in real time$qb$, $qb$Adequate — monitoring occurs but not in real time — issues are often identified too late$qb$, $qb$Weak — monitoring happens only when an issue becomes publicly visible$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation does not monitor for brand threats$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q04$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the leadership communication capability during reputational challenges is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — leaders are trained and communicate clearly, calmly, and credibly under pressure$qb$, $qb$Adequate — leaders manage communications reasonably well in challenging situations$qb$, $qb$Weak — some leaders communicate well under pressure; others worsen reputational damage$qb$, $qb$Very poor — leadership communication in difficult situations tends to amplify rather than resolve damage$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q05$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the proactive management of reputational risk is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — the organisation actively identifies and mitigates reputation risks before they materialise$qb$, $qb$Adequate — risk management processes address most reputation risks proactively$qb$, $qb$Weak — reputation risks are typically addressed only after they materialise$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation takes limited proactive steps to prevent reputational damage$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q06$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the cybersecurity incident management as a reputational risk is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — cybersecurity is treated as a reputation risk with robust incident response plans$qb$, $qb$Adequate — cybersecurity awareness and basic incident response capability exist$qb$, $qb$Weak — cybersecurity risk to reputation has not been fully assessed or planned for$qb$, $qb$Very poor — cybersecurity as a reputation risk is not on the agenda$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q07$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the supplier and partner conduct risk management is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — supplier conduct is reviewed as part of due diligence and monitored ongoing$qb$, $qb$Adequate — major suppliers are screened but the process is not comprehensive$qb$, $qb$Weak — supplier conduct risk is acknowledged but not systematically managed$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation does not consider supplier conduct as a reputation risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q08$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the legal and compliance risk as a brand threat is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — legal and compliance issues are assessed for brand impact before they escalate$qb$, $qb$Adequate — legal issues are managed by legal without explicit brand impact consideration$qb$, $qb$Weak — brand impact of legal issues is considered only when they become public$qb$, $qb$Very poor — legal and compliance risks are not assessed for their brand impact$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q09$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the social media risk management is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — policies and monitoring are in place for employee and organisational social media conduct$qb$, $qb$Adequate — a social media policy exists but enforcement is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Weak — some guidance exists but social media risk is not actively managed$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation has no social media risk management$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q10$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the overall crisis management and reputation risk capability is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — the organisation is prepared for and resilient against reputational threats — a genuine strength$qb$, $qb$Adequate — basic crisis management capability exists and would be adequate for most scenarios$qb$, $qb$Weak — reputation risk management gaps create material vulnerability$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation is significantly exposed to reputational risk with inadequate preparation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C09Q01$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the consistency and quality of customer experience is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — customers consistently describe the experience as better than expected — it is a brand differentiator$qb$, $qb$Adequate — customers are generally satisfied with the experience they receive$qb$, $qb$Weak — experience quality varies significantly depending on the team, channel, or timing$qb$, $qb$Very poor — a significant proportion of customers have negative experiences that damage the brand$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
